  "why": "Every event after the snapshot is about a neighbouring question: 1D finite CA, 1D liftings, injectivity complexity, infinite alphabets, linear CA over groups. All are variant. The one close call is Durand-Lose 2001, which says in its abstract that it expresses every d-dimensional reversible CA as d+1 block permutations. Arrighi and Nesme (2010) say that construction enlarges the alphabet. Wikipedia's block CA article says it uses more states. Kari (2005, 2009, as cited by Wikipedia), Arrighi–Nesme and Eppstein all still call the d≥3 question open after 2001. So I read it as simulation by a different automaton, which is a changed question (variant), not as making the given automaton locally reversible (whole). Had I scored it whole, the problem would read open_stale rather than open. Kari 1996 settles only d=1 and 2, which the statement (d≥3) excludes, so it is variant. Kari 1999 is likewise an embedding/layer result, which I answered variant. The 2608.20600 AI declaration is not in ai.systems and concerns another problem, so its AI role is none.",
  "doubts": "The Durand-Lose body was never read, so the enlarged-alphabet reading rests on secondary sources. They agree with each other, and expert status statements after 2001 support them. 'Locally reversible' is undefined in the statement. For the old journal papers, verification was set to digested on the basis of journal publication.",
